is_anomaly: true
faulty_devices: srv1
diagnosis_text:
Affected component: Campus DNS service on srv1 (BIND/named). The daemon is running, but it is configured as an authoritative-only server with recursion disabled.

Mechanism: PC1 (and likely other clients) use srv1 as their resolver (pc1 /etc/resolv.conf → nameserver 10.5.0.4). When a user browses the Internet, queries for external names are sent to srv1. Because BIND is set to “recursion no”, it refuses those queries, so lookups for sites like www.google.com are answered with REFUSED. Applications then stall while retrying/falling back (for example to DoH), so pages “hang” and then “catch up,” which feels like a slow/overloaded Internet link even though the transport path is fine.

Localization and evidence:
- srv1 /etc/bind/named.conf shows “recursion no;” and only an internal zone defined.
- From r1: dig @10.5.0.4 www.google.com returns status: REFUSED (recursion requested but not available).
- From pc1: nslookup www.google.com 10.5.0.4 → REFUSED.
- Transport is healthy: pc1→ispa RTT ~1.4 ms; pc1↔srv1 iperf ~95–109 Mbps; topology-wide pings clean.

Root cause: BIND on srv1 was intentionally or inadvertently configured as non-recursive, but clients are pointing at it for general Internet DNS.

Corrective fix: Reconfigure BIND on srv1 as a recursive caching resolver for campus clients. In /etc/bind/named.conf options: set “recursion yes;” and “allow-recursion { 10.0.0.0/8; };” and, if required by the lab design, define appropriate forwarders (or stub zones) to an upstream recursive resolver; then reload/restart named. With recursion enabled, external DNS lookups will resolve promptly and the “slow Internet” symptoms will disappear without changing WAN links.
=== END ===